<<~ ahu #/orientation>>
! Art Sigils — Formal Analysis

Interpret a visible artwork before imagining what instructions might reproduce its properties. The image supplies evidence of appearance, not direct access to the maker's mental state or technical workflow. Reverse engineering yields a plausible specification, not certain knowledge of the original prompt, hidden layers, intention, or medium.

Foundational lines: Erwin Panofsky's pre-iconographical description, iconographical identification, and iconological interpretation; Getty CDWA `ofness` versus `aboutness`; Feldman's description-analysis-interpretation-judgment educational model; Project Zero's observation-to-evidence question; Arnheim's visual organization; Baxandall's historically situated inference; Kress and van Leeuwen's representation/interaction/composition.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/method>>
!! Looking protocol

Keep separate columns of thought: direct observation, contextual identification, interpretive hypothesis, and proposed creative reconstruction. Never promote an inference into an observation merely because the reading feels persuasive.

# **Describe:** visible subject, crop, aspect, orientation, regions, literal inscriptions, people, objects, media artifacts and graphic elements; avoid premature story inference.
# **Organize:** dominant mass, focal point, figure/ground, value keys, hue/chroma, local contrast, silhouette, line, spatial recession, edge hierarchy, scale, rhythm and negative space.
# **Relate:** which parts overlap, correct, obscure, quote, contradict, or echo others? For an image of images distinguish outer support, inner studies, and fictional scene contents.
# **Read:** describe plausible first/second/later reads; include an alternative reading, and note how composition warrants each.
# **Situate:** what work type and viewer relationship appear plausible? Map, poster, editorial plate, book illustration, devotional image, study wall, production sheet? Viewer addressed need not be represented.
# **Materially inspect:** distinguish depicted paper from depicted landscape, substrate from marks, texture from gloss, atmosphere from universal image noise. Describe *appearances* rather than asserting unobserved tools.
# **Interpret:** offer symbolic, narrative, stylistic, iconographic and contextual hypotheses, separating evidence from source-dependent lore.
# **Evaluate:** articulate what succeeds, which feature obstructs purpose, why, and possible revision; do not equate technical neatness with artistic merit.
# **Reverse-specify:** translate observed relations into modular procedure calls; explicitly label observed, inferred, uncertain, and intentionally unspecified features.
# **Counter-read:** seek a reasonable alternate identity or purpose; preserve unresolved interpretations rather than manufacturing certainty.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/vocabulary>>
!! Formal analytic registers

* **Elements:** line, shape, form, colour, value, texture, space.
* **Organization:** scale, proportion, emphasis, balance, rhythm, hierarchy, repetition, variation, unity, contrast, movement, adjacency.
* **Edge:** hard, soft, lost, found; their local distribution changes attention and depth.
* **Paint/drawing:** gesture, contour, hatching, scumbling, wash, impasto, glazing, granulation, smudge, reserve, correction, overpainting; only assert physical technique when evidence supports it.
* **Spatial construction:** horizon, vanishing behavior, foreshortening, aerial perspective, plan oblique, planimetric map, panorama, occlusion, generalization.
* **Visual narrative:** implied before/after, story event, open action, iconographic identification, image quotation, metapicture, implied audience, artifact function.
* **Production:** thumbnail, colour key, material study, silhouette pass, route sketch, rejected alternative, correction overlay, resolution study, presentation art.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/reverse-example>>
!! Compact worked reverse-analysis

Observed: a large pale mountain painting partly covered by a translucent line drawing. The overlay's river bends differently from the underlying painting. A charcoal sketch near the edge shows a dark mineral shape that resembles a bone. No person stands in the depicted studio.

Possible reading: the board communicates unresolved geographical and archaeological questions. The overlay may challenge the painted river; the charcoal study may argue for a skeletal identity. The image cannot establish the maker's original medium or intention from pixels alone.

Candidate invocations (illustrative only):

```tiddlywiki
<<art-work name:"Mountain Research Board" kind:"image of pinned visual studies" purpose:"present provisional interpretations of one fictional region">>
<<art-address audience:"art collaborators" relationship:"visual review from outside the picture" invitation:"compare conflicting studies">>
<<art-depict subject:"two overlapping terrain pictures and one relic sketch" setting:"physical pinboard" location:"artists and audience outside scene; any traveler figures only inside the represented artworks">>
<<art-dispute study-a:"painted mountain" study-b:"hydrographic overlay" disagreement:"river course" visible-proof:"two clearly different river lines">>
<<art-infer interpretation:"the dark form may suggest a giant bone" evidence:"curved hollow silhouette in a charcoal sketch" alternative:"mineral deposit or broken mechanism">>
```
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pitfalls>>
!! Common misreadings

* **Intent laundering:** observed beauty does not prove a specific conscious design objective.
* **Attribution inflation:** resemblance to a named artist does not establish authorship, actual technique, or direct reference use.
* **Iconographic overreach:** visually bone-like does not mean an identified mythological figure.
* **Medium overclaim:** pixels resembling gouache need not originate from physical gouache.
* **Level confusion:** the viewer of a depicted pinboard does not stand physically in the picture; tiny travelers may appear in an inner landscape drawing.
* **Texture collapse:** identical fine-scale marks on clouds, fabric, stone, paper and metal warrant separate observation, not the unsupported diagnosis of a particular model mechanism.
* **Decorative critique:** 'more dynamic' lacks an observable change; specify which mass, edge, focal contrast or relation needs alteration.
* **False map precision:** a cinematic panorama does not automatically provide a metric route or drainage chart.
<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/references>>
!! Authorities and further reading

* Getty CDWA Subject Matter, especially **ofness/aboutness**, evidence and ambiguity: https://www.getty.edu/publications/categories-description-works-art/categories/object-architecture-group/16/
* Getty AAT and Materials/Techniques: https://www.getty.edu/research/conducting_research/vocabularies/aat/ ; https://www.getty.edu/publications/categories-description-works-art/categories/object-architecture-group/7/
* Harvard Project Zero, What Makes You Say That?: https://pz.harvard.edu/resources/what-makes-you-say
* Harvard Project Zero, See, Think, Wonder: https://pz.harvard.edu/resources/see-think-wonder
* Panofsky, Studies in Iconology and Meaning in the Visual Arts; Rudolf Arnheim, Art and Visual Perception; Michael Baxandall, Patterns of Intention; Kress and van Leeuwen, Reading Images; W. J. T. Mitchell, Picture Theory. These books supply overlapping lenses, not a single infallible method.
* Jenny and Patterson, Introducing Plan Oblique Relief: https://cartographicperspectives.org/index.php/journal/article/view/cp57-jenny-patterson
